#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unit Testing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:11
msgid ""
"In our current testing environment, we spend a significant amount of effort "
"crafting end-to-end tests for error conditions that could easily be captured "
"by unit tests (or we simply forgo some hard-to-setup and rare error "
"conditions).  Unit tests additionally provide stability to the codebase and "
"can simplify debugging through isolation. Writing unit tests in pure C, "
"rather than with our current shell/test-tool helper setup, simplifies test "
"setup, simplifies passing data around (no shell-isms required), and reduces "
"testing runtime by not spawning a separate process for every test "
"invocation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:14
msgid ""
"We believe that a large body of unit tests, living alongside the existing "
"test suite, will improve code quality for the Git project."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:22
msgid ""
"For the purposes of this document, we'll use *test framework* to refer to "
"projects that support writing test cases and running tests within the "
"context of a single executable. *Test harness* will refer to projects that "
"manage running multiple executables (each of which may contain multiple test "
"cases) and aggregating their results."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:25
msgid ""
"In reality, these terms are not strictly defined, and many of the projects "
"discussed below contain features from both categories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:29
msgid ""
"For now, we will evaluate projects solely on their framework features. Since "
"we are relying on having TAP output (see below), we can assume that any "
"framework can be made to work with a harness that we can choose later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:36
msgid ""
"We believe the best way forward is to implement a custom TAP framework for "
"the Git project. We use a version of the framework originally proposed in "
"https://lore.kernel.org/git/c902a166-98ce-afba-93f2-ea6027557176@gmail.com/[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:39
msgid ""
"See the <<framework-selection,Framework Selection>> section below for the "
"rationale behind this decision."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing a test harness"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:46
msgid ""
"During upstream discussion, it was occasionally noted that `prove` provides "
"many convenient features, such as scheduling slower tests first, or "
"re-running previously failed tests."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:52
msgid ""
"While we already support the use of `prove` as a test harness for the shell "
"tests, it is not strictly required. The t/Makefile allows running shell "
"tests directly (though with interleaved output if parallelism is "
"enabled). Git developers who wish to use `prove` as a more advanced harness "
"can do so by setting DEFAULT_TEST_TARGET=prove in their config.mak."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:56
msgid ""
"We will follow a similar approach for unit tests: by default the test "
"executables will be run directly from the t/Makefile, but `prove` can be "
"configured with DEFAULT_UNIT_TEST_TARGET=prove."
msgstr ""

