#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:83
#, no-wrap
msgid "License"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:88
msgid ""
"We must be able to legally use the framework in connection with Git. As Git "
"is licensed only under GPLv2, we must eliminate any LGPLv3, GPLv3, or Apache "
"2.0 projects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vendorable or ubiquitous"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:97
msgid ""
"We want to avoid forcing Git developers to install new tools just to run "
"unit tests. Any prospective frameworks and harnesses must either be "
"vendorable (meaning, we can copy their source directly into Git's "
"repository), or so ubiquitous that it is reasonable to expect that most "
"developers will have the tools installed already."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:99
#, no-wrap
msgid "Maintainable / extensible"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:106
msgid ""
"It is unlikely that any pre-existing project perfectly fits our needs, so "
"any project we select will need to be actively maintained and open to "
"accepting changes. Alternatively, assuming we are vendoring the source into "
"our repo, it must be simple enough that Git developers can feel comfortable "
"making changes as needed to our version."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:112
msgid ""
"In the comparison table below, \"True\" means that the framework seems to "
"have active developers, that it is simple enough that Git developers can "
"make changes to it, and that the project seems open to accepting external "
"contributions (or that it is vendorable). \"Partial\" means that at least "
"one of the above conditions holds."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "Major platform support"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:117
msgid "At a bare minimum, unit-testing must work on Linux, MacOS, and Windows."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:121
msgid ""
"In the comparison table below, \"True\" means that it works on all three "
"major platforms with no issues. \"Partial\" means that there may be "
"annoyances on one or more platforms, but it is still usable in principle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "TAP support"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:129
msgid ""
"The https://testanything.org/[Test Anything Protocol] is a text-based "
"interface that allows tests to communicate with a test harness. It is "
"already used by Git's integration test suite. Supporting TAP output is a "
"mandatory feature for any prospective test framework."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:133
msgid ""
"In the comparison table below, \"True\" means this is natively supported.  "
"\"Partial\" means TAP output must be generated by post-processing the native "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:136
msgid ""
"Frameworks that do not have at least Partial support will not be evaluated "
"further."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "Diagnostic output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:143
msgid ""
"When a test case fails, the framework must generate enough diagnostic output "
"to help developers find the appropriate test case in source code in order to "
"debug the failure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "Runtime-skippable tests"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:149
msgid ""
"Test authors may wish to skip certain test cases based on runtime "
"circumstances, so the framework should support this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parallel execution"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:156
msgid ""
"Ideally, we will build up a significant collection of unit test cases, most "
"likely split across multiple executables. It will be necessary to run these "
"tests in parallel to enable fast develop-test-debug cycles."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:160
msgid ""
"In the comparison table below, \"True\" means that individual test cases "
"within a single test executable can be run in parallel. We assume that "
"executable-level parallelism can be handled by the test harness."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mock support"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:168
msgid ""
"Unit test authors may wish to test code that interacts with objects that may "
"be inconvenient to handle in a test (e.g. interacting with a network "
"service).  Mocking allows test authors to provide a fake implementation of "
"these objects for more convenient tests."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "Signal & error handling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:174
msgid ""
"The test framework should fail gracefully when test cases are themselves "
"buggy or when they are interrupted by signals during runtime."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "Project KLOC"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:181
msgid ""
"The size of the project, in thousands of lines of code as measured by "
"https://dwheeler.com/sloccount/[sloccount] (rounded up to the next multiple "
"of 1,000). As a tie-breaker, we probably prefer a project with fewer LOC."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:183
#, no-wrap
msgid "Adoption"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/technical/unit-tests.txt:187
msgid ""
"As a tie-breaker, we prefer a more widely-used project. We use the number of "
"GitHub / GitLab stars to estimate this."
msgstr ""

